93. SEO Strategy
Develop an SEO strategy appropriate to the organisation, its audience and its market.
Consider relevant search intent and the actual information visitors are seeking.
94. Search Keyword Selection
Use relevant keywords naturally.
Do not invent keyword volumes, competition levels, ranking data or traffic forecasts.
If research tools are unavailable, distinguish informed recommendations from verified keyword research.
95. Page Metadata
Provide appropriate:
Page titles.
Meta descriptions.
Canonical URLs where necessary.
Language declarations.
Robots directives.
Social sharing metadata.
Metadata should accurately describe each page.
96. Heading Structure
Use a logical heading hierarchy.
Do not choose heading levels solely because of their visual appearance.
97. URL Structure
Use readable, descriptive URLs where the platform allows.
Avoid unnecessary parameters and confusing URL structures.
98. Internal Linking
Create useful internal links that help visitors discover relevant information.
Avoid unnecessary or misleading links.
99. Image SEO
Use descriptive filenames and meaningful alternative text.
Optimise images for quality and performance.
100. Structured Data
Implement appropriate structured data where relevant.
Use accurate schema types and genuine information.
Do not include fabricated reviews, ratings, prices or business details.
101. Local SEO
For location-based organisations, consider:
Accurate business name.
Address.
Telephone number.
Opening hours.
Location information.
Appropriate local business structured data.
Relevant geographical content.
Never invent local business information.
102. Technical SEO
Where appropriate, provide:
XML sitemap.
Robots.txt.
Canonical handling.
Appropriate indexing directives.
Correct redirects.
Clean internal linking.
Suitable page status codes.
Do not block important pages from search engines unintentionally.
103. Search Engine Tools
Where relevant, prepare for integration with:
Google Search Console.
Bing Webmaster Tools.
Google Analytics or another analytics platform.
