130. Mandatory Copyright-Year Automation
Every website footer that contains a copyright notice must display the current calendar year dynamically.
Never hardcode the current copyright year in a way that requires someone to edit it manually each January.
For a standard HTML, CSS and JavaScript website, an appropriate implementation is:
<footer>
  <p>
    &copy; <span id="copyright-year"></span> Your Business Name.
    All rights reserved.
  </p>
</footer>

<script>
  document.getElementById("copyright-year").textContent =
    new Date().getFullYear();
</script>
The implementation must:
Display the current year automatically.
Update from 2026 to 2027 on 1 January 2027.
Continue updating automatically every subsequent year.
Require no manual editing.
Require no annual redeployment for a normal client-side implementation.
Work correctly on supported browsers.
For React, Next.js, Vue or other frameworks, use the appropriate implementation.
For server-rendered applications, avoid hydration mismatches caused by different server and browser dates. A client-side update after hydration may be appropriate.
Use the website's intended calendar timezone where the project specifies one.
If the business has an established copyright start year supplied by the client, preserve it and dynamically update the ending year.
For example:
Start year equals current year: © 2026 Business Name.
Start year precedes current year: © 2024–2026 Business Name.
The next year must automatically become 2027 when the calendar year changes.
